f constraints.\nL17: If p is fixed, that is, independent of the input size, the algorithm makes use of efficient classical preprocessing. If p grows with the input size a different strategy is proposed. We study the algorithm as applied to MaxCut on regular graphs and analyze its performance on 2-regular and 3-regular graphs for fixed p. For p = 1, on 3-regular graphs the quantum algorithm always finds a cut that is at least 0.6924 times the size of the optimal cut.\nL18: Subjects:  | Quantum Physics (quant-ph)\nL19: Report number:  | MIT-CTP/4610\nL20: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:1411.4028】 [quant-ph]\nL21:    | (or 【11†arXiv:1411.4028v1】 [quant-ph] for this version)\nL22:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1411.4028†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Edward Farhi [【13†view email】]\nL26: [v1] Fri, 14 Nov 2014 19:57:57 UTC (22 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled A Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm, by Edward Farhi and 2 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL35:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL36:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL37: \nL38: 【15†view license】\nL39: \nL40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: quant-ph\nL43: \nL44: 【16†< prev】   |   【17†next >】\nL45: \nL46: 【18†new】 | 【19†recent】 | 【20†2014-11】\nL47: ### References & Citations\nL48: \nL49:   * 【21†INSPIRE HEP†inspirehep.net】\nL50:   * 【22†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu】\nL51:   * 【23†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL52:   * 【24†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL53: \nL54: ### 【25†6 blog links】\nL55: \nL56: (【26†what is this?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL71: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Expl