rimental projects with community collaborators\nL148: \nL149: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL150: \nL151: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL152: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? 【42†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org】.\nL153: \nL154: 【43†Which authors of this paper are endorsers?】 | Disable MathJax (【44†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL155: \nL156: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, 【45†member institutions†info.arxiv.org】, , and all contributors.\nL157: 【46†About†info.arxiv.org】 · 【47†Help†info.arxiv.org】 · 【48†Contact†info.arxiv.org】 · 【49†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org】 · 【50†Copyright†info.arxiv.org】 · 【51†Privacy†info.arxiv.org】 · 【52†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org】 · 【53†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org】\nL158: \nL159: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1709.03489] From the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm to a Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz (https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.03489)\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.03489\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 160\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Quantum Physics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 11 Sep 2017 (【5†v1】), last revised 28 Feb 2019 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:From the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm to a Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Stuart Hadfield】, 【7†Zhihui Wang】, 【8†Bryan O'Gorman】, 【9†Eleanor G. Rieffel】, 【10†Davide Venturelli】, 【11†Rupak Biswas】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled From the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm to a Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz, by Stuart Hadfield and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【12†View PDF】 【13†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:The next few years will be exciting as prototype universal quantum processors emerge, enabling implementation of a wider variety of algorithms. Of particular interest are quantum heuristics, which require experimentation on quantum hardware for their evaluation, and which have the potential to significantly expand the breadth of quantum computing applications.\nL17: A leading candidate is Farhi et al.'s Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm, which alternates between applying a cost-function-based Hamiltonian and a mixing Hamiltonian. Here, we extend this framework to allow alternation between more general families of operators.\nL18: The essence of this extension, the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz, is the consideration of general parametrized families of unitaries rather than only those corresponding to the time-evolution under a fixed local Hamiltonian for a time specified by the parameter. This ansatz supports the representation of a larger, and potentially more useful, set of states than the original formulation, with potential long-term impact on a broad array of application areas.\nL19: For cases that call for mixing only within a desired subspace, refocusing on unitaries rather than Hamiltonians enables more efficiently implementable mixers than was possible in the original framework. Such mixers are particularly useful for optimization problems with hard constraints that must always be satisfied, defining a feasible subspace, and soft constraints whose violation we wish to minimize.\nL20: More efficient implementation enables earlier experimental exploration of an alternating operator approach to a wide variety of approximate optimization, exact optimization, and sampling problems. Here, we introduce the Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz, lay out design criteria for mixing operators, detail mappings for eight problems, and provide brief descriptions of mappings for diverse problems.\nL21: Comments:  | 51 pages, 2 figures. Revised to match journal paper\nL22: Subjects:  | Quantum Physics (quant-ph)\nL23: Cite as:  | 【14†arXiv:1709.03489】 [quant-ph]\nL24:    | (or 【15†arXiv:1709.03489v2】 [quant-ph] for this version)\nL25:    | 【16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1709.03489†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: Journal reference:  | Algorithms 12.2 (2019): 34. (Special issue \"Quantum Optimization Theory, Algorithms, and Applications\")\nL27: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | 【17†https://doi.org/10.3390/a12020034†doi.org】 DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL28: ## Submission history\nL29: \nL30: From: Stuart Hadfield [【18†view email】]\nL31: 【5†[v1]】 Mon, 11 Sep 2017 17:50:49 UTC (74 KB)\nL32: [v2] Thu, 28 Feb 2019 10:33:52 UTC (157 KB)\nL33: \nL34: Full-text links:\nL35: \nL36: ## Access Paper:\nL37: \nL38: View a PDF of the paper titled From the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm to a Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz, by Stuart Hadfield and 5 other authors\nL39: \nL40:   * 【12†View PDF】\nL41:   * 【13†HTML (experimental)】\nL42:   * 【19†TeX Source】\nL43: \nL44: 【20†view license】\nL45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: quant-ph\nL48: \nL49: 【21†< prev】   |   【22†next >】\nL50: \nL51: 【23†new】 | 【24†recent】 | 【25†2017-09】\nL52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * 【26†INSPIRE HEP†inspirehep.net】\nL56:   * 【27†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL57:   * 【28†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL58:   * 【29†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL61: \nL62: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL63: \nL64: [Button: ×]\nL65: \nL66: loading...\nL67: \nL68: Data 